;;; One candidate, refuted in TWO firings — **W2**, the forced-positive cascade
;;; refuting a consistent program, at **default flags**. Regression
;;; `forced-2` (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── The program ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One object. `(q one)` taints it, a taint is a contradiction, so `(q one)`
;;; dies — in two firings, one past what the pre-branch lookahead simulates.
;;; `:no-hypothesis (bad)` keeps the rung off the taint itself.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; Without any hypothesis the program is consistent, and the one hypothesis
;;; there is dies. Root is the solution (Q-M1e.6, clause 3): **`Solution`,
;;; k = 1, a model with no `q` fact.**
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered before `fbcae3b2` ─────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   solutions (k)   0
;;;   verdict         No solution — the constraints are contradictory
;;;   unsat core (0 facts)
;;;   exhausted        true
;;;   enterings        0
;;;
;;; The lookahead could not see a two-firing death, so `alive = {(q one)}`, a
;;; singleton, and `promote_forced_positives` wrote `(q one)` into **root** with
;;; a premise-less `<forced-positive>` provenance. Root was then contradictory,
;;; `root_dead` refuted the run, and the refutation was certified with no fact
;;; behind it. No commitment was ever entered.
;;;
;;; ── Twins ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `forced-2-off.ein` is this program, the twin that turned the cascade off:
;;; there the walk still answered `Contradiction`, through W1 at layer 0, with a
;;; 1-fact core, so fixing W2 alone fell through into W1. `forced-1.ein` is the
;;; one-firing twin, right at default flags and wrong the same way under `-L`.
;;; Now R is {∅}, root alone (`ein solve FILE`).
;;;
;;;
;;;
(relation thing T :why "{?1} is a thing")
(relation q T :why "q holds of {?1}")
(relation bad T :why "{?1} is bad")
(thing one)
(rule taint () :match (q ?x)   :assert (bad ?x) :why "q holds of {?x}, which taints it: {?x} is bad")
(rule kill  () :match (bad ?x) :assert (false) :why "{?x} is bad, which cannot be")
(solve :no-hypothesis (bad))
(query :goal (thing ?x))
